well a national fundraising campaign has  been launched tonight to help ukraine  the disasters emergency committee is  appealing for funds to help people  affected by the conflict we're joined  now to discuss that with david miliband  the president and ceo of the  international rescue committee and jan  agarland general secretary of the  norwegian refugee council very good to  talk to you both gentlemen and i'd like  you both first of all just we need to  pause don't we and reflect on the speed  and scale of what's going on here david  miller banned one million people in a  week  yes the  scale of this is obviously huge it's on  a par with the syria crisis  of uh ten years ago uh six and a half  million refugees in the end from  syria but by  the standards of the syria crisis it  took three months for a million syrians  to flee from their own country it's  taken just a week to hit that number and  of course what's going on in inside  ukraine is even more  challenging than the situation once you  cross the borders because you've shown  throughout the day the pictures of the  bombed out buildings there are civilians  there and there are obviously at least a  million probably more civilians on the  run inside ukraine trying to head for  the kind of safety that you've just  showcased from lev so this really is a  capstone for the modern age of impunity  it shows what happens when the rule of  law becomes optional and when the rights  of civilians  are disregarded by an invading power  yeah nagaland i see you nodding along  there in agreement of course in just on  those numbers a million people equate to  something like two percent of the entire  population of ukraine in eight days  indeed it is uh explosive i was myself  in ukraine last month february  it's traveled up and down what was then  the front line in eastern ukraine  the  let's remember there are millions who  cannot also escape  in the eastern part of the country where  my organization has been most active  there is a lot of elderly people  pensioners retired miners they're widows  they cannot flee they are disabled etc  so what we're calling for is a truce a a  humanitarian force so that we can resume  humanitarian operations so that the  civilian population can get out of  harm's way it will be the only sensible  thing and i understand it was discussed  today  between the envoys of ukraine and russia  well let's uh discuss that more than  david miliband i mean there's a very big  difference as you both know better than  most between  discussing safe corridors and actually  getting them to work implementing them  they were of course tried many times in  syria to let's say not great effect  well i think that we do have a lot of  experience of the denial of humanitarian  aid as well as the bombardment of  civilians and i'm afraid that the syria  example is one example but jan eglin  as well as you derma will know well the  lesson of the 1990s in the former  yugoslavia in europe where siege tactics  were used extensively where  indiscriminate bombing was used  extensively and where the international  system was massively challenged now i've  just been on the zoom core with our team  in poland across the border from ukraine  we are already identifying partner  organizations  both in poland and inside ukraine  there's going to be great strain on the  health system and on pharmaceutical  supplies as well as on the food and  water infrastructure and one lesson that  we do know from the 90s and from the  syria campaign is establishing those  routes for aid early is absolutely key  of course that doesn't uh absolve the  politicians or the politics and the  politics can get in the way as it is  today in other countries around the  world like yemen or ethiopia where  aid is being denied but i think there  are some practical steps that are being  taken by the ngos who are part of this  disasters emergency committee campaign  as well as by the un institutions and  that's absolutely vital if we're to  avoid or help some people avoid the  worst of this because jan is right there  will be lots of people who can't get out  and some people who are injured or even  killed on the way out and we've got to  be concerned about the situation inside  ukraine as well as the european response  across the border and yanagalan what  about the humanitarian colleagues you  have on the ground in great numbers  given the nature of the russian attack  they're in great danger as well  they are and that's why we're calling  for this  truce today this uh boss  directly to the kremlin directly to the  parties i i have 70  colleagues in the norwegian refugee  council on the ground inside of ukraine  but at the moment they are fleeing for  their life they are spending most of the  time in the  in the bomb shelter  we have 40 colleagues in severe donetsk  which is in the east there is too little  attention to these most journalists are  like your good colleagues in the western  ukraine  uh the casualties up are you know  mostly in the east the it's it's  horrific the fighting uh there and there  is also where the  elderly the pensioners the  most disabled are so really we need an  end to this madness we need an end to  the assault on civilian cities we need  horses we need this humanitarian  corridors and we need to do  like david just  mentioned we need then  as we all are setting up operations in  neighboring countries and doing  cross-border relief into ukraine most of  those in need are still in ukraine they  very few 1 million has left another 10  20 million are in harm's way well can i  ask you a bit more though about that the  million that have left and presumably uh  the many hundreds of thousands let's  hope it's not too many more that that  follow and the reception they get david  miller and of course the neighboring  countries mainly eu countries are  dealing with with the brunt of those  numbers and just tell me uh your view on  the reception that they're getting there  the eu announcing that they're going to  have ukrainians will receive residence  permits access to housing healthcare  education and very important when it  comes to being a refugee  the right to enter the labor market do  you think that all european countries  should offer this kind of reception  yes i do i think that the information  that we have  is that europe is stepping up day by day  and it's stepping up better and  better remember  there probably will be millions more i  think that the uh un has estimated up to  five million refugees but the european  continent is 500 million people so let's  bear that in mind as we think about the  logistical and integration challenge  from my point of view it's essential  that every eu country european union  country all 27 of them do offer the same  rights and they've committed to do that  on the way that you've in the way that  you've described however it's also very  important that other european countries  i'm thinking of the uk i'm thinking of  norway i'm thinking of switzerland i  think i'm  absolutely clear that those european  countries need to step up in the same  way and it's a source of enormous  sadness to me that the uk has not yet  joined that european mainstream in  committing that ukrainians whatever  their ties to the country they will be  able to find  safe haven and they will be able to  support themselves through work remember  this is an educated population that are  coming  out and that they will be able to find  services for their families i think it's  very very important that europe shows  not just that it's able to defend itself  but that also it's able to defend its  values and that's vital in this conflict  okay  i've got a last question for you both  because i can't have um people with your  experience on without exploring this  issue and it is of course the best way  to end the suffering is to end the  violence is to end the war and you're  both deeply involved in diplomacy and in  other lives in other careers and young  england i mean of course you were deeply  involved in so many peace processes of  course some notably the the oslo accords  the the historic agreement between  israel and the plo  i mean i mean do you see anything like  that any any way of de-escalating this  fairly soon  it has to be very clear that this can  end tomorrow i mean president putin can  end it tomorrow  if this is not uh  this is not syria with the hundreds of  armed groups or  the congo with  half a thousand armed groups it's two  armies it's an attacking army the  russian army can't end it  and there has to be  another a  big big effort by diplomats  to to get the leaders the russians to  their census then of course we also have  to recognize that it didn't get better  in afghanistan or in syria or in yemen  because it got worse in in ukraine we  also have to be as equally generous to  the  people freeing from there as we are now  to ukraine and there is a is an enormous  outpouring of of sympathy solidarity  with ukraine we welcome that let's also  remember all of the other refugees  fleeing from elsewhere and david  miliband your thoughts on the diplomacy  of course with your experience as  foreign secretary of meeting putin  meeting lavrov seeing them up close and  personal negotiating with them  i mean looking at their mindset now is  there any way of deflecting them from  further extreme violence  derma i don't want to mislead you or  your viewers  president putin spoke to president  macron of france today and the french  readout was that they fear the worst and  frankly i fear the worst now as well i  fear a pulverization campaign on the  cities of ukraine i fear for the  civilians of ukraine whether they be in  their bomb shelters or whether they be  on the  on the run and this is now  on a very very dangerous course there is  only one side that can end this and that  is the russian  side but i think it would be giving  false hope to your viewers to pretend  that there's any sign of that at the  moment what we do know  is that there is unprecedented pressure  on the russian system as a result of the  economic sanctions and other  economic interventions that have taken  place and there is unprecedented protest  inside russia as well incredibly brave  uh people but that's where the change is  gonna have to come and it would be wrong  to pretend that there's a diplomatic fix  to get out of that well gentlemen thank  you both so much for sharing your  expertise with this david miliband jan  eggerland very good talking to you thank
 

